] characterized the extremal graphs with the minimum (reverse) cover cost of trees with some given graphs parameters.\nChen [9], Chen and Zhang [10] and Huang and Li [17] provided the explicit formulae for the hitting times on some types of interesting graphs, such as trees, unicyclic graphs, subdivision graphs, triangulation graphs and quadrilateral graphs. For more advances on the hitting times of random walks on graphs, one may be referred to [2], [4], [18], [26], [28] and the references cited therein.\n\nThe cover cost [15] of a vertex u in G is defined as C{C}_{G}\\left(u\\right)=\\sum _{v\\in {V}_{G}}{H}_{G}(u,v).This parameter was first proposed by Georgakopoulos as a tractable variant of the cover time. The cover cost is closely related to the cover time of a graph. The cover time {\\tau }_{\\mathrm{cov}} of \\left\\{{X}_{k}\\right\\}\\phantom{\\rule{0.16667em}{0ex}}(k⩾0) is the minimum number of steps the random walk takes to reach that all the vertices: {\\tau }_{\\mathrm{cov}}=inf\\{k⩾0:{X}_{k}=y\\text{ for all }y\\in {V}_{G}\\}.The expected value of {\\tau }_{\\mathrm{cov}} when the walk is started at vertex {v}_{i} is denoted by {\\mathbb{E}}_{i}{\\tau }_{\\mathrm{cov}}.\n\nAnalogously, the reverse cover cost [16] of a vertex u in G is defined as R{C}_{G}\\left(u\\right)=\\sum _{v\\in {V}_{G}}{H}_{G}(v,u).These two parameters have been extensively studied by probabilists, combinatorialists, physicists etc. Georgakopoulos and Wagner [16] first proved that C{C}_{T}\\left(u\\right) (resp. R{C}_{T}\\left(u\\right)) is minimized by the star whose root is a leaf (resp. a centre), and maximized by the path whose root is a centre (resp. a leaf) among all rooted trees. Huang et al. [18] determined the minimum and the maximum (reverse) cover cost of unicyclic graphs. Zhang and Li [32] characterized the extremal graphs with the minimum (reverse) cover cost of trees with some given graphs parameters. Li and Wang [20] considered the extremal problems on the (reverse) cover cost of trees with given segment sequence (see below). For more advances on these two parameters one may be referred to [16] and in its references cited therein.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJournal of Applied Analysis and Computation (https://www.jaac-online.com/data/article/jaac/preview/pdf/20180616.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Qi, Eigenvalue analysis of constrained minimization problem for ... Su, Enumerating closed ﬂows on forks, Disc.\n[73] V. De Silva, L. Lim. Tensor rank and the ill-posedness of the best low-rank\napproximation problem, SIAM J. Matrix Anal. Appl., 2008, 30(3), 1084–1127.\n[74] Y. Song, L. Qi, Necessary and suﬃcient conditions for copositive tensors, Lin-\near & Multilinear Algebra, 2015, 63(1), 120–131.\n[75] Y. Song, L. Qi, Strictly semi-positive tensors and the boundedness of tensor\ncomplementarity problems, Optim. Letters, 2017, 11(7), 1407–1426.\n[76] Y. Song, L. Qi, Tensor complementarity problem and semi-positive tensors, J\nOptim. Theory Appl., 2016, 169, 1069–1078.\n[77] Y. Song, L. Qi, Eigenvalue analysis of constrained minimization problem for\nhomogeneous polynomials, J. Global Optim., 2016, 64, 563–575.\n[78] X. Su, Enumerating closed ﬂows on forks, Disc. Math., 2017, 340, 3002–3010.\n[79] X. Su, The truncated determinants of combinatorial rectangular arrays, Ars\nCombinatoria, 2017, 133, 205–216.\n[80] F. Sun, L. Liu, Y. Wu, Finite time blow-up for a thin-ﬁlm equation with initial\ndata at arbitrary energy level, J. Math. Anal. Appl., 2018, 458, 9–20.\n[81] M. Sun, Y. Wang, J. Liu, Generalized Peaceman-Rachford splitting method for\nmultiple-block separable convex programming with applications to robust PCA,\nCalcoco, 2017, 54, 77–94.\n[82] G. Wang, Existence-stability theorems for strong vector set-valued equilibrium\nproblems in reﬂexive Banach space, J. Ineq. Appl., 2015, 2015, 239.\n[83] X. Wang, Alternating proximal penalization algorithm for the modiﬁed multiple-\nsets split feasibility problems, J. Ineq. Appl., 2018, 2018, 48.\n[84] X. Wang, H. Chen, Y. Wang, Solution structures of tensor complementarity\nproblem, Front. Math. China, 2017, Doi: 10.1007/s11464-018-0675-2.\n[85] Y. Wang, Louis Caccetta, G. Zhou, Convergence analysis of a block improve-\nment method for polynomial optimization over unit spheres, Numer. Linear\nAlgebra & Appl., 2015, 22, 1059–1076.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHitting times of random walks on unicyclic graphs with a given number of pendant vertices (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0166218X25004597)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Brightwell and Winkler [4] proved that among all n$n$-vertex graphs, the maximum expected hitting time is attained in the “lollipop graph”. ... Li, Wang, Zhang [15], [16], [17], [24] and Huang [9] investigated extremal problems related to cover cost and reverse cover cost. ... Let broom graph {B}_{n,p}${B}_{n,p}$ is a tree consisting of: a path (with n-p+1$n-p+1$ vertices), and a star (a central vertex connected to p-1$p-1$ leaves), where one endpoint of the path is identified with the center of the star (see Fig.\nIn particular, extremal problems of random walks on graphs have received a lot of attention. Brightwell and Winkler [4] proved that among all n$n$-vertex graphs, the maximum expected hitting time is attained in the “lollipop graph”. In [3], they derived the extremal results for cover times on trees. Georgakopoulos and Wagner [8] exhibited a close connection between hitting times of the simple random walk on a graph, the Wiener index, and related graph invariants. They determined the extremal values of hitting time, cover cost, and reverse cover cost for trees of a given order. Beveridge and Youngblood [2] showed that among all n$n$-vertex trees, mixing time is minimized on the star, and maxim